| Manual processes      | Admissions only        |         ⚠️ |        ❌ |                  ❌ |          ❌ |                                  Variable |

### The biggest thing I learned from this comparison

**Atlas isn't competing in an empty market.**

There are already cheap-ish systems that can do admissions **and much more**.

That doesn't mean Atlas is doomed. It means your positioning has to be extremely precise:

> **Atlas is not the cheapest college-management system.**
> **Atlas is not an ERP.**
> **Atlas is a specialised admissions platform for institutions that want to modernise admissions without replacing their existing college-management systems.**

And this actually makes your **standalone/no-ERP-integration boundary** more understandable.

A college that already has its academic/finance/student system can potentially use Atlas purely for:

**Recruitment/application → documents → admissions review → decision → acceptance**

and leave everything after that to its existing system.

That's the market gap I'd investigate rather than trying to beat STRATMIS on feature count.

**One warning:** for MySpace-Edu, MyEncore, eduSYMS and ITS, I cannot give you a credible exact rand price from public information because they don't publish a comparable college price. I would rather tell you **“custom quote”** than make up a number. ([MySpace Edu][2])

